────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#1053 is fixed and committed as b4bd5438. If evidence.md or .yoyo.toml exists but can't be read, yoyo now refuses and names the file. Before, it treated the file as empty and wrote over it. fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full cargo test run all pass.

The second writer was real. Before changing anything, I tested /config set against a .yoyo.toml containing one invalid byte (\xe9). It returned Ok and rewrote the file. So the bug wasn't only in /todo board evidence: the shared reader built for #1047 had the same flaw.

What changed:
• `src/non_regular_read.rs`: read_for_rewrite now returns an empty string only when the file doesn't exist.
  • A FIFO or device is still refused with the same message as before.
  • Any other read error (bad UTF-8, permission denied) now returns an error saying could not read <path> (<error>); it was left untouched, nothing written, with the path cleaned for display.
  • The doc comment records the old behaviour as superseded, and why.
• `src/commands_todo.rs`: /todo board evidence uses read_for_rewrite now. When the read fails it prints a red "Did not add evidence: …" and writes nothing.
• `src/config.rs`: not edited. Both config writers already pass the error up, and the code that calls them already prints it in red.

I fixed it in the shared reader rather than adding a wrapper around it, so the evidence command and both config writers are all covered.

Tests: I wrote these first and watched them fail before the fix.
• read_for_rewrite returns an error on a non-UTF-8 file.
• Both config writers leave a non-UTF-8 .yoyo.toml exactly as it was, checked byte for byte.
• An unreadable evidence.md is left exactly as it was, and the reply is not "Added evidence".
• Near miss: a readable evidence.md still gets the new line appended exactly as before.
• The existing test for missing and regular files is unedited and still passes.

Check that the tests catch the bug: I temporarily put the old catch-all back (every error became an empty string), ran the tests and restored the file in one command. Exactly the three tests on unreadable files failed by name. Nothing from that experiment is left in the tree.

Other: git grep '#1053' only finds code comments and test docs, so no message a user sees points at the ticket that this push will close. I added an entry to ARCHITECTURE.md under src/non_regular_read.rs, noting the policy change and that /config set was a second affected writer.
